{"body":{"text":["What a browser keeps is written so a load cut short never leaves a torn blob: a new file is written under another name beside it and moved into place once whole, and a file already there, as a head is, is swapped whole by the browser. An empty file under the name of bytes that are not empty, as an earlier write left when cut short, is no blob kept: it is let go and fetched again; and a blob that cannot be kept, its name held by a file another worker keeps open, is read from the bytes fetched while the page lives. Before, one such file stopped the page from opening at all (20/36). *The session's, at the author's word that the work be thorough (20/35); built.*"]},"id":"1M84FACXX4FE000","instance":["1M6YBY3G0000002"]}